---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dashboard from '../components/Dashboard.astro';
import SpacePanel from '../components/SpacePanel.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Aurora Tonight at My Location: Go or No-Go"
  description="Check tonight's aurora australis chances for your own spot in Australia or New Zealand. Uses your coordinates for cloud, darkness and distance from the aurora."
  path="/my-location/"
>
  <h1>Aurora tonight at your own spot</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    For anywhere in Australia or New Zealand that is not on the <a href="/forecast/">list of places</a>. Share your
    location, or type coordinates, and the same go or no-go is worked out for that spot.
  </p>

  <section class="card" data-aurora="custom">
    <p>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-slot="locate">Use my location</button>
    </p>
    <form>
      <div class="formrow">
        <label>Latitude <input type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="-42.88" data-slot="lat" /></label>
        <label>Longitude <input type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="147.33" data-slot="lon" /></label>
        <button class="btn quiet" type="submit">Check these</button>
      </div>
    </form>
    <p class="small muted" data-slot="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="small" data-slot="facts" hidden></p>
    <p>
      <button class="btn quiet" type="button" data-slot="forget" hidden>Forget my spot</button>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Your coordinates are rounded to two decimal places, about 1 km, and kept only in this browser. They are sent to
      this site's own server to fetch the cloud forecast, and nowhere else. <a href="/privacy/">Privacy details</a>.
    </p>
    <noscript><p class="notice">This page needs JavaScript to read your location and load live data.</p></noscript>
  </section>

  <Dashboard hidden />

  <h2>Space weather right now</h2>
  <SpacePanel />

  <h2>How your spot is judged</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The page looks up the magnetic latitude of your coordinates from a grid computed with the AACGM-v2 model, then
      applies the same rule as every other page: the aurora's edge has to come within 5 degrees for a display you can
      see. Darkness and the moon are calculated for your exact spot, and the cloud forecast is for the nearest point
      in MET Norway's model.
    </p>
    <p>
      Times on this page are shown in your device's time zone. <a href="/how-it-works/">More on the method</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
